      (text "Last Saturday\nwebsite for the nuj prc site map up to span of 7 and ply of 3\nYou can find the NUJ PR & Communications website here. If you're looking for a specific site map with a span of 7 and ply of 3, could you clarify what you mean by those parameters? Are you referring to a structural layout, a mapping convention, or something else? Happy to help refine the search!\n\nCan you explain what 'span' and 'ply' mean in site maps?\nGreat question! In the context of site maps, span and ply are terms that can relate to different aspects of structure and hierarchy:\n\nSpan typically refers to the breadth or width of a site map's structure—how many branches or sections extend from a particular node. If a site map has a span of 7, it could mean that a given level has seven main categories or pages branching out from a central parent.\n\nPly is often used to describe depth—how many levels exist within the hierarchy. A site map with a ply of 3 would mean there are three layers of navigation, ...")
