      (id "imp-0753")
      (date "2025-06-05T19:24:30")
      (text "2.	The Guidance: newsgathering techniques \n1.	Contact within the initial 48 hour period following an attack\n2.1	Journalists play a vital role in informing the public in the aftermath of a terror attack. This is also the most sensitive time for survivors and the bereaved. Wherever possible we would encourage journalists( if they want to make contact with victims within the first 48 hours to do so via third party routes, such as the police or victim support organisations. Where indirect approaches are not possible and direct contact is seen as essential, approaches should be coordinated to avoid multiple enquiries.\n2.2	Where a victim proactively makes contact with the media in the first 48 hours following an attack, the media should respect any decision by the victim to change their mind about speaking to the media. \n2.	Contact after 48 hours and beyond \n2.3	Victims should not be put under pressure to speak to reporters.  \n2.4	Attempts to contact victims should be approached with s...")
      (truncated #t))
    (comment
      (id "imp-0754")
      (date "2025-06-05T19:23:29")
      (text "2.10	Reporters should not approach victims in/outside hospitals or where they are otherwise receiving medical care. \n2.11	The interests of children warrant special consideration both in law and under the relevant media codes. Victims under the age of 18 should not be interviewed, questioned or photographed unless a custodial parent or similarly responsible adult consents and is present.\n3.	The Guidance: reporting on terror attacks \nRevealing deaths\n3.1	As with newsgathering, media organisations should not (through publication or broadcast) inform people of the death of a family member. Therefore care must be taken to ensure that close family are already aware of a family member's death before this is made public. \n3.2	This is consistent with Clause 4 of the IPSO Editor's Code and Clause 8 of the OFCOM Broadcasting Code, as well as College of Policing Guidance. \n\nPhotographs/footage \n\n3.3	Whilst the media often publish photographs taken from social media, careful thought should...")
      (truncated #t))
    (comment
      (id "imp-0755")
      (date "2025-06-05T19:22:43")
      (text "PRIVILEGED AND CONFIDENTIAL\n	Draft Guidance on media reporting of terror attacks\n1.	Introduction\n1.1	Media organisations play a key role in reporting on terror attacks – a role which is unquestionably in the public interest. Media reporting helps us understand what happened, why it happened and who might be held responsible. It also gives victims opportunities to explain the impact on their family and pay public tribute to loved ones.\n1.2	Alongside this role there is also a responsibility to the victims and survivors, including their privacy rights. The appropriate balance will vary from case to case. We know of no journalist who seeks to cause further harm by their reporting, but harm can nevertheless happen given the circumstances and the scale of media interest, unless good practice is followed.\n1.3	This Guidance follows the publication of a report commissioned by Survivors Against Terror titled \"A Second Trauma: Media reporting of terror attacks through the eyes of survivors\...")
      (truncated #t))
