      (id "imp-0765")
      (date "2025-06-05T17:45:27")
      (text "This DM recognises the increasingly toxic and stigmatising media framing of disability, including neurodivergence, and stigmatising language about minority groups.  DM notes the link between stigmatising media portrayal of minority groups and hate crimes, as well as policies that limit disabled people’s access to support.  This DM notes that in rejecting NUJ’s and other organisations’ calls to extend Clause 12 (Discrimination) of the IPSO Editors Code from being targeted at named individuals to groups in its 2023 review, IPSO’s Editors Code of Practice Committee partly justified the refusal by pointing to NUJ’s Code of Conduct Clause 9 that it claimed also does not cover discrimination against groups.  DM is concerned that others may feel the NUJ’s own Code of Conduct falls short on discrimination and could be used to justify the lack of recourse for minority groups against discriminatory media coverage, despite discrimination against groups being recognised under the Equality Act 2010...")
      (truncated #t))
    (comment
      (id "imp-0766")
      (date "2025-06-05T17:44:20")
      (text "129 SOC recommends the following motion is out of order being not in accordance with the facts, a code of guidance exists for Public Relations.  This DM believes that the commitment to freedom of expression, honesty, accuracy and professional ethics espoused in the NUJ Code of Conduct are values that all NUJ members should be proud to sign up to, regardless of which sector of the media they work in.  It notes, however, that while the NUJ Code of Conduct is designed specifically to relate to the work of members engaged in news gathering and reporting, and serves that purpose well, there is currently no broader set of principles and ethics that applies to NUJ members across all sectors, including, for instance, Book publishing and Public Relations and Communications.  DM believes that developing such a set of principles would be of value in associating the NUJ ‘brand’ with principles of professionalism, honesty and integrity, and would promote a stronger sense of shared identity between ...")
      (truncated #t))
